1
00:00:10,060 --> 00:00:16,060
Hi, you're watching lecture number 5 of the Introduction to Photonics video series.

2
00:00:16,060 --> 00:00:20,060
Today we are going to talk about the optical components.

3
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:24,060
So we are slowly moving in the direction of doing practical stuff.

4
00:00:24,060 --> 00:00:29,060
So the concepts and the subject that is going to be introduced today

5
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:35,060
is going to play an important role in your hands-on part of this course

6
00:00:35,060 --> 00:00:37,060
that you're going to take at the end of it.

7
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:43,060
So again, direction number 5, we are going to introduce the optical components.

8
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:49,060
So specifically, we are going to talk about the thermal, mechanical and chemical properties of optical materials

9
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:54,060
in addition to their primary optical characteristics.

10
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:56,060
So we talked a little bit about optical materials.

11
00:00:56,060 --> 00:01:04,060
Last time we explained and expanded on the topic of index of refraction.

12
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:08,060
Today we're going to also, as I said, look at some other non-optical properties

13
00:01:08,060 --> 00:01:13,060
that are playing an important role in the selection of optical materials

14
00:01:13,060 --> 00:01:17,060
such as thermal, mechanical and chemical properties.

15
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:20,060
We're also going to look at different optical materials

16
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:25,060
and we'll see how we can choose a specific type of optical material

17
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:31,060
for a specific use in different types of spectral regions

18
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:34,060
such as visible ultraviolet and infrared region.

19
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:38,060
And finally, we are going to shift our attention at the end

20
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:42,060
to different types of coatings and filters.

21
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:52,060
We'll see that very often coatings are being placed on a transparent material for different reasons.

22
00:01:52,060 --> 00:01:58,060
And we're going to explain different processes to achieve that.

23
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:01,060
And finally, we're going to talk about different types of filters

24
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:04,060
that you can come across in a photonics lab.

25
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:07,060
In addition to optical properties of optical materials,

26
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:12,060
there's also other non-optical properties that have to be taken into account.

27
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:17,060
Specifically, we are going to talk first about thermal properties of optical materials.

28
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:21,060
Extreme heat or cold and rapid changes in temperature

29
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:29,060
can cause temporary or permanent changes in the physical characteristics of an optical material.

30
00:02:29,060 --> 00:02:32,060
So the goal is to select the optical material

31
00:02:32,060 --> 00:02:36,060
where these types of changes are reduced to the minimum.

32
00:02:36,060 --> 00:02:38,060
Why is this important to us?

33
00:02:38,060 --> 00:02:42,060
Very often you are going to take a specific type of optical component

34
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:45,060
made out of optical material.

35
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:48,060
Most of the time it's going to be some sort of glass.

36
00:02:48,060 --> 00:02:51,060
And in order to establish an optical system,

37
00:02:51,060 --> 00:02:54,060
you are going to mount these optical components,

38
00:02:54,060 --> 00:03:03,060
be it prisms or optical windows or lenses or mirrors made out of glass.

39
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:10,060
You're going to mount them into some sort of optical mount that is going to be made out of metal.

40
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:16,060
So that's a specific example where thermal properties of both materials

41
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:19,060
would start playing an important role.

42
00:03:19,060 --> 00:03:20,060
Why?

43
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:27,060
Because the thermal expansion of glass and thermal expansion of metal are not going to be the same.

44
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:31,060
So if your optical system, if your optical component in the system

45
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:35,060
is exposed to variations in the temperature,

46
00:03:35,060 --> 00:03:39,060
those variations in temperature may result,

47
00:03:39,060 --> 00:03:45,060
may cause thermal expansion of glass and metal at different rates,

48
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:51,060
which in turn is going to result in a specific amount of stress

49
00:03:51,060 --> 00:03:58,060
on the optical elements that can be serious to the point where the optical component can even break.

50
00:03:58,060 --> 00:04:01,060
So once more, if a lens is held in a metallic mount,

51
00:04:01,060 --> 00:04:04,060
the combination is subjected to a high temperature,

52
00:04:04,060 --> 00:04:07,060
the primary heat loss that's going to be experienced by the lens

53
00:04:07,060 --> 00:04:10,060
will be through its edges and onto the mount.

54
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:14,060
This will make the window hotter at the center and cooler at the edges.

55
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:17,060
And not only that you're introducing the stress,

56
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:23,060
this condition can also create certain distortion in optical properties to optical material

57
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:30,060
that can significantly have an impact on the performance of your optical system.

58
00:04:30,060 --> 00:04:35,060
The table on the right-hand side shows thermal properties of some of the typical materials

59
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:39,060
that we are coming across in a photonic slab.

60
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:44,060
You can see a few different types of metals such as copper, silver and steel.

61
00:04:44,060 --> 00:04:48,060
And then down on the bottom you have different crystals and glasses

62
00:04:48,060 --> 00:04:55,060
that would be different types of transparent materials that optical components are made out of.

63
00:04:55,060 --> 00:04:58,060
There's a few things that you can immediately observe.

64
00:04:58,060 --> 00:05:04,060
You can see that, for example, the thermal conductivity of metals is significantly larger

65
00:05:04,060 --> 00:05:10,060
than the thermal conductivity of glass-based types of materials.

66
00:05:10,060 --> 00:05:14,060
In other words, if these materials are heated,

67
00:05:14,060 --> 00:05:20,060
it's going to be much easier to take the heat away from the metals

68
00:05:20,060 --> 00:05:24,060
as opposed to the glasses that are going to get hotter.

69
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:31,060
Also, the last column in the table shows the amount of thermal expansion

70
00:05:31,060 --> 00:05:33,060
of each of these materials.

71
00:05:33,060 --> 00:05:38,060
And you can see that the metals are expanding at much larger rates

72
00:05:38,060 --> 00:05:41,060
than most of the crystals and glasses.

73
00:05:41,060 --> 00:05:50,060
So if both metals and transparent materials are exposed to the temperature variations,

74
00:05:50,060 --> 00:05:53,060
you're going to see a significant thermal expansion of the metals,

75
00:05:53,060 --> 00:06:00,060
which may, as we already elaborated, which may cause the stress on the joints

76
00:06:00,060 --> 00:06:02,060
between the metal and the glass.

77
00:06:02,060 --> 00:06:06,060
There are certain ways how you can quantify the stress

78
00:06:06,060 --> 00:06:09,060
that's caused through the thermal expansion.

79
00:06:09,060 --> 00:06:13,060
So here's an example of an equation that can be used.

80
00:06:13,060 --> 00:06:18,060
So if you want to figure out the stress,

81
00:06:18,060 --> 00:06:26,060
that stress can be quantified through a certain relationship

82
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:29,060
between the thickness of the optical component,

83
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:33,060
the diameter of the component,

84
00:06:33,060 --> 00:06:37,060
pressure difference between the sides of the optical component,

85
00:06:37,060 --> 00:06:40,060
elasticity of the material, et cetera.

86
00:06:40,060 --> 00:06:46,060
So we are not going to go much in detail about implementing the formula

87
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:47,060
that you are seeing here.

88
00:06:47,060 --> 00:06:50,060
This formula, this equation is just given for reference

89
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:53,060
to show you that you can use certain concepts

90
00:06:53,060 --> 00:07:00,060
from a thermodynamic to quantify the amount of stress

91
00:07:00,060 --> 00:07:05,060
that can be induced in the case of significant thermal variations

92
00:07:05,060 --> 00:07:11,060
on materials with different coefficients of expansion.

93
00:07:11,060 --> 00:07:14,060
In addition to thermal properties,

94
00:07:14,060 --> 00:07:19,060
chemical properties of optical materials can also affect

95
00:07:19,060 --> 00:07:22,060
the performance of your optical system

96
00:07:22,060 --> 00:07:26,060
and would have to be paid close attention.

97
00:07:26,060 --> 00:07:29,060
So we're talking about the chemical damage to optical element

98
00:07:29,060 --> 00:07:34,060
that can be caused by different chemicals

99
00:07:34,060 --> 00:07:39,060
or chemical ability to etch or dissolve on a transparent medium.

100
00:07:39,060 --> 00:07:45,060
So examples of such damage can be the effects of acetone

101
00:07:45,060 --> 00:07:48,060
or plastics, as well the effects of water vapor

102
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:55,060
on ionic crystal windows such as different types of salt.

103
00:07:55,060 --> 00:08:00,060
So this is another important aspect that has to be taken to account

104
00:08:00,060 --> 00:08:02,060
if you're working in the photonic slab

105
00:08:02,060 --> 00:08:05,060
based on the experiments that you are performing.

106
00:08:05,060 --> 00:08:12,060
So if you are using certain chemicals for different experimentations,

107
00:08:12,060 --> 00:08:16,060
you have to pay close attention what is going to be the effect

108
00:08:16,060 --> 00:08:21,060
of those chemicals on different optical windows, prisms,

109
00:08:21,060 --> 00:08:27,060
lenses, and other materials that you may be using in your optical lab.

110
00:08:27,060 --> 00:08:31,060
Everything that we talked about so far about optical materials

111
00:08:31,060 --> 00:08:35,060
and optical components is going to have a significant impact

112
00:08:35,060 --> 00:08:40,060
on your decision or if you are tasked

113
00:08:40,060 --> 00:08:45,060
to select specific optical components for your optical system.

114
00:08:45,060 --> 00:08:49,060
So as a photonics technician, very often you will be tasked

115
00:08:49,060 --> 00:08:53,060
with a task of establishing a specific optical system

116
00:08:53,060 --> 00:08:57,060
that is going to also involve selection

117
00:08:57,060 --> 00:09:00,060
and the order of different types of optical components.

118
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:08,060
So the prior knowledge of the concepts that we are introducing

119
00:09:08,060 --> 00:09:13,060
in this and the previous lecture is going to play an important role

120
00:09:13,060 --> 00:09:20,060
in the success of how you are executing that task of selection

121
00:09:20,060 --> 00:09:23,060
and the order of optical components.

122
00:09:23,060 --> 00:09:27,060
So again, to wrap up, to summarize,

123
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:29,060
when you are selecting an optical component,

124
00:09:29,060 --> 00:09:32,060
you have to pay close attention to the optical properties

125
00:09:32,060 --> 00:09:36,060
of the materials that your component is going to be made out of.

126
00:09:36,060 --> 00:09:41,060
And not only optical properties, but also thermal properties, chemical properties.

127
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:44,060
And finally, also the cost.

128
00:09:44,060 --> 00:09:47,060
So very often you are going to be dealing with a limited budget,

129
00:09:47,060 --> 00:09:52,060
so you have to make a good decision as far as the quality

130
00:09:52,060 --> 00:09:54,060
of the optical components that you are ordering

131
00:09:54,060 --> 00:09:58,060
and as well as the pricing of these components.

132
00:09:58,060 --> 00:10:02,060
So the cost of optical components will depend on many factors

133
00:10:02,060 --> 00:10:04,060
that you have to take into account.

134
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:07,060
For example, if you're looking at the glass and fused quartz,

135
00:10:07,060 --> 00:10:12,060
those types of components are comparatively expensive

136
00:10:12,060 --> 00:10:16,060
because these materials have to be grounded and polished

137
00:10:16,060 --> 00:10:20,060
and then tested and maintained for a correct flatness of curvature.

138
00:10:20,060 --> 00:10:24,060
So in this case, if you are ordering glass

139
00:10:24,060 --> 00:10:26,060
or fused quartz type of optical components,

140
00:10:26,060 --> 00:10:28,060
those would be the most expensive.

141
00:10:28,060 --> 00:10:32,060
You also have some cheaper alternatives such as plastic optics

142
00:10:32,060 --> 00:10:35,060
because in the case of a plastic optics,

143
00:10:35,060 --> 00:10:37,060
you don't have to do the polishing,

144
00:10:37,060 --> 00:10:42,060
but rather you can make them using the molding processes.

145
00:10:42,060 --> 00:10:45,060
Also, if you're looking at optical elements that are made out of crystals,

146
00:10:45,060 --> 00:10:48,060
they are also expensive because these crystals have to be grown

147
00:10:48,060 --> 00:10:51,060
in a large size with expensive equipment.

148
00:10:51,060 --> 00:10:54,060
So pretty much the type of process that's being used

149
00:10:54,060 --> 00:10:58,060
to manufacture a specific type of a material or a component

150
00:10:58,060 --> 00:11:03,060
is going to play an important role in the cost of those components.

151
00:11:03,060 --> 00:11:06,060
So knowing a little bit about different materials

152
00:11:06,060 --> 00:11:10,060
and how a specific type of optical component is being made

153
00:11:10,060 --> 00:11:12,060
would definitely not hurt,

154
00:11:12,060 --> 00:11:15,060
and it's going to help you make a sound decision

155
00:11:15,060 --> 00:11:17,060
about a specific optical component

156
00:11:17,060 --> 00:11:20,060
that you're going to be buying for your photonic slab.

157
00:11:20,060 --> 00:11:23,060
We also have to keep in mind that most of these optical components

158
00:11:23,060 --> 00:11:27,060
would have a specific type of coating

159
00:11:27,060 --> 00:11:29,060
that's going to be placed on the optical component

160
00:11:29,060 --> 00:11:31,060
for different reasons,

161
00:11:31,060 --> 00:11:35,060
such as anti-reflective coatings, anti-transmission coatings, et cetera,

162
00:11:35,060 --> 00:11:38,060
and all those processes of applying the coating

163
00:11:38,060 --> 00:11:42,060
would also increase the cost of these elements considerably.

164
00:11:42,060 --> 00:11:46,060
So to summarize, the manufacturing cost of an optical element

165
00:11:46,060 --> 00:11:48,060
depends on many factors,

166
00:11:48,060 --> 00:11:50,060
size of the component,

167
00:11:50,060 --> 00:11:53,060
type of a material that's being used,

168
00:11:53,060 --> 00:11:55,060
surface curvature of the element,

169
00:11:55,060 --> 00:11:59,060
it's easier to obviously make a flat component

170
00:11:59,060 --> 00:12:02,060
if you have to make some sort of correct component, such as lens.

171
00:12:02,060 --> 00:12:05,060
Accuracy of the flatness of the curvature

172
00:12:05,060 --> 00:12:07,060
is going to play an important role.

173
00:12:07,060 --> 00:12:10,060
Of course, it depends on the optical system that you're building.

174
00:12:10,060 --> 00:12:14,060
You know how the accuracy of the flatness or curvature is important

175
00:12:14,060 --> 00:12:17,060
is crucial for the performance of your optical system.

176
00:12:17,060 --> 00:12:19,060
You also have to take into account

177
00:12:19,060 --> 00:12:21,060
the wavelength of the light to be transmitted.

178
00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:23,060
Different materials would be reacting

179
00:12:23,060 --> 00:12:26,060
to different types of wavelengths in a different way.

180
00:12:26,060 --> 00:12:29,060
And finally, the types of coatings on the surface,

181
00:12:29,060 --> 00:12:35,060
and finally the aberrations that may be on the optical component

182
00:12:35,060 --> 00:12:37,060
that would have to be corrected.

183
00:12:37,060 --> 00:12:40,060
So all these different factors are going to play an important role

184
00:12:40,060 --> 00:12:43,060
in establishing the cost of the optical component

185
00:12:43,060 --> 00:12:45,060
and how much you're going to pay for them.

186
00:12:45,060 --> 00:12:47,060
So as a photonics technician,

187
00:12:47,060 --> 00:12:50,060
you have to know all these different aspects to a certain extent

188
00:12:50,060 --> 00:12:52,060
to be able to make a sound decision

189
00:12:52,060 --> 00:12:54,060
during the selection process

190
00:12:54,060 --> 00:12:57,060
of the components for the optical system that you're building.

191
00:12:57,060 --> 00:12:59,060
So what you're seeing on this slide

192
00:12:59,060 --> 00:13:02,060
are different types of optical components

193
00:13:02,060 --> 00:13:05,060
that you may come across in a photonics lab.

194
00:13:05,060 --> 00:13:07,060
Most of them, if not all,

195
00:13:07,060 --> 00:13:09,060
are made out of transparent materials,

196
00:13:09,060 --> 00:13:15,060
the glass, fuse quartz, plastic optics.

197
00:13:15,060 --> 00:13:21,060
And the shape would determine the type of the optical component,

198
00:13:21,060 --> 00:13:24,060
so you may come across the front surface mirrors,

199
00:13:24,060 --> 00:13:28,060
you may come across filters, lenses, prisms, et cetera.

200
00:13:28,060 --> 00:13:31,060
Many of these components are also going to have

201
00:13:31,060 --> 00:13:33,060
a specific type of optical coating.

202
00:13:33,060 --> 00:13:37,060
You can see that certain surfaces of these components

203
00:13:37,060 --> 00:13:39,060
are shown here in different colors,

204
00:13:39,060 --> 00:13:42,060
and those colors are actually representing the optical coating

205
00:13:42,060 --> 00:13:46,060
that has been placed on an optical component.

206
00:13:46,060 --> 00:13:50,060
So we are talking about materials of a specific thickness

207
00:13:50,060 --> 00:13:54,060
and composition that are deposited through a certain process

208
00:13:54,060 --> 00:13:56,060
on the surface of the optical components

209
00:13:56,060 --> 00:13:59,060
to control the amount of light that's passing through them.

210
00:13:59,060 --> 00:14:03,060
So these optical coatings have a very, very, very

211
00:14:03,060 --> 00:14:08,060
tightly controlled thickness through specific processes

212
00:14:08,060 --> 00:14:10,060
because the thickness of the optical coating

213
00:14:10,060 --> 00:14:13,060
is going to affect how that optical component

214
00:14:13,060 --> 00:14:17,060
is going to be performing in the optical system.

215
00:14:17,060 --> 00:14:19,060
In other words, you know how it is going to control

216
00:14:19,060 --> 00:14:22,060
the reflection and the transmission of the light

217
00:14:22,060 --> 00:14:26,060
as the light is being passed through that optical coating.

218
00:14:26,060 --> 00:14:30,060
This light gives you a brief insight into the two

219
00:14:30,060 --> 00:14:35,060
distinct processes that are being used in the photonics

220
00:14:35,060 --> 00:14:39,060
to deposit or to apply an optical coating

221
00:14:39,060 --> 00:14:41,060
onto an optical component.

222
00:14:41,060 --> 00:14:46,060
So those two processes are evaporation and sputtering.

223
00:14:46,060 --> 00:14:48,060
In the case of evaporation, the material that's

224
00:14:48,060 --> 00:14:51,060
to be deposited is converted into a vapor

225
00:14:51,060 --> 00:14:53,060
using a thermal or electronic beam,

226
00:14:53,060 --> 00:14:56,060
and then that vapor is going to be depositing itself

227
00:14:56,060 --> 00:14:59,060
onto the optical material substrate.

228
00:14:59,060 --> 00:15:03,060
So there has to be some sort of control mechanism

229
00:15:03,060 --> 00:15:06,060
as far as the duration of the whole deposition

230
00:15:06,060 --> 00:15:12,060
because the longer you continue with this process,

231
00:15:12,060 --> 00:15:17,060
the thickness of the optical coating is going to increase.

232
00:15:17,060 --> 00:15:21,060
So there has to be some sort of dynamic control

233
00:15:21,060 --> 00:15:24,060
or measurement of the thickness that is going to stop

234
00:15:24,060 --> 00:15:29,060
the whole process when the optical coating

235
00:15:29,060 --> 00:15:32,060
achieves the desired thickness.

236
00:15:32,060 --> 00:15:34,060
The second process is so-called sputtering.

237
00:15:34,060 --> 00:15:38,060
In this case, we are talking about the use of a high voltage

238
00:15:38,060 --> 00:15:40,060
that creates a heavy ions of plasma,

239
00:15:40,060 --> 00:15:44,060
and that plasma is going to bombard so-called target

240
00:15:44,060 --> 00:15:46,060
and knock out small particles of the target

241
00:15:46,060 --> 00:15:50,060
that are then going to be condensed on a cooled substrate.

242
00:15:50,060 --> 00:15:53,060
So the optical coating is a material

243
00:15:53,060 --> 00:15:56,060
that has been knocked out of the target

244
00:15:56,060 --> 00:15:58,060
placed in a sputter.

245
00:15:58,060 --> 00:16:01,060
And then, as I said, heavy ions of plasma

246
00:16:01,060 --> 00:16:03,060
are going to be hitting that target

247
00:16:03,060 --> 00:16:06,060
and knocking out small particles of the material

248
00:16:06,060 --> 00:16:09,060
that is going to be then applied

249
00:16:09,060 --> 00:16:12,060
in the form of a coating on the optical component.

250
00:16:12,060 --> 00:16:16,060
Both of these processes are being used in optics

251
00:16:16,060 --> 00:16:18,060
to create optical coatings.

252
00:16:18,060 --> 00:16:21,060
And in addition to the process of application,

253
00:16:21,060 --> 00:16:23,060
you also, as I mentioned,

254
00:16:23,060 --> 00:16:26,060
have to have some sort of thickness control

255
00:16:26,060 --> 00:16:31,060
in a dynamic means where the thickness

256
00:16:31,060 --> 00:16:35,060
of the layer of coating that's being deposited

257
00:16:35,060 --> 00:16:38,060
through these two processes is going to be controlled

258
00:16:38,060 --> 00:16:41,060
during the actual process of application.

259
00:16:41,060 --> 00:16:44,060
And when the thickness of the coating,

260
00:16:44,060 --> 00:16:49,060
as I said, achieves a desired value,

261
00:16:49,060 --> 00:16:52,060
the whole process would have to stop.

262
00:16:52,060 --> 00:16:53,060
As we already mentioned,

263
00:16:53,060 --> 00:16:56,060
the thickness control of the optical coating

264
00:16:56,060 --> 00:16:58,060
is extremely important

265
00:16:58,060 --> 00:17:00,060
because the thickness of the coating

266
00:17:00,060 --> 00:17:03,060
is going to affect its optical properties.

267
00:17:03,060 --> 00:17:07,060
So during the process of optical coating application,

268
00:17:07,060 --> 00:17:09,060
you have to have some sort of way

269
00:17:09,060 --> 00:17:11,060
of monitoring the thickness

270
00:17:11,060 --> 00:17:14,060
while the optical component is in the chamber.

271
00:17:14,060 --> 00:17:17,060
There's two distinct ways how is this being achieved.

272
00:17:17,060 --> 00:17:20,060
The first one is the use of so-called crystal oscillators.

273
00:17:20,060 --> 00:17:23,060
These crystal oscillators are based on the quartz crystal plates

274
00:17:23,060 --> 00:17:25,060
that are placed in the same plane

275
00:17:25,060 --> 00:17:27,060
as the substrate to be coated.

276
00:17:27,060 --> 00:17:30,060
It turns out that the resonant frequency

277
00:17:30,060 --> 00:17:34,060
of oscillation of such an oscillator

278
00:17:34,060 --> 00:17:36,060
is going to depend on the mass of the crystal.

279
00:17:36,060 --> 00:17:39,060
So as you are applying the coating

280
00:17:39,060 --> 00:17:41,060
onto the optical component,

281
00:17:41,060 --> 00:17:43,060
its mass is going to increase,

282
00:17:43,060 --> 00:17:46,060
and that increase is going to result

283
00:17:46,060 --> 00:17:48,060
in the change of the resonant frequency

284
00:17:48,060 --> 00:17:50,060
of the crystal oscillator.

285
00:17:50,060 --> 00:17:52,060
And that's going to be the mechanism

286
00:17:52,060 --> 00:17:54,060
how you are controlling the thickness

287
00:17:54,060 --> 00:17:58,060
of the optical component, of the optical coating.

288
00:17:58,060 --> 00:18:03,060
Another way is the use of so-called interference effects.

289
00:18:03,060 --> 00:18:09,060
So we are observing interference effects

290
00:18:09,060 --> 00:18:12,060
through a transmitted or reflected optical beam,

291
00:18:12,060 --> 00:18:16,060
and the transmittance obviously is going to change

292
00:18:16,060 --> 00:18:18,060
as the thickness of the coating layer is building up,

293
00:18:18,060 --> 00:18:21,060
and that is going to establish the mechanism

294
00:18:21,060 --> 00:18:22,060
of how we are going to be controlling

295
00:18:22,060 --> 00:18:25,060
the thickness of the optical coating.

296
00:18:25,060 --> 00:18:28,060
Now that we introduced the process

297
00:18:28,060 --> 00:18:32,060
of the optical coating application,

298
00:18:32,060 --> 00:18:34,060
and the way how it's being controlled,

299
00:18:34,060 --> 00:18:37,060
let's talk about a few different types

300
00:18:37,060 --> 00:18:39,060
of optical coatings that you may come across

301
00:18:39,060 --> 00:18:43,060
in the world of optics.

302
00:18:43,060 --> 00:18:46,060
The first one is so-called anti-reflection coating.

303
00:18:46,060 --> 00:18:48,060
This is a very popular type of coating,

304
00:18:48,060 --> 00:18:51,060
very often is being put on a glass,

305
00:18:51,060 --> 00:18:54,060
as you can see on the right-hand side.

306
00:18:54,060 --> 00:18:57,060
So what we are trying to achieve here

307
00:18:57,060 --> 00:19:00,060
is a reduction of the reflection.

308
00:19:00,060 --> 00:19:03,060
So when the light hits this transparent optical medium

309
00:19:03,060 --> 00:19:05,060
that's made out of glass,

310
00:19:05,060 --> 00:19:08,060
as we already explained in the previous lecture,

311
00:19:08,060 --> 00:19:11,060
there's going to be a certain amount of reflection

312
00:19:11,060 --> 00:19:14,060
off of the surface of the glass,

313
00:19:14,060 --> 00:19:17,060
based on the indices of reflection of the medium

314
00:19:17,060 --> 00:19:19,060
that's surrounding the glass

315
00:19:19,060 --> 00:19:23,060
and the index of reflection of the glass itself.

316
00:19:23,060 --> 00:19:27,060
So the way how the coating is going to reduce the reflection

317
00:19:27,060 --> 00:19:30,060
is a very creative way.

318
00:19:30,060 --> 00:19:32,060
So if you're looking at what's shown here

319
00:19:32,060 --> 00:19:34,060
on the left-hand side,

320
00:19:34,060 --> 00:19:36,060
you can see here basically three layers.

321
00:19:36,060 --> 00:19:38,060
The first layer is the air,

322
00:19:38,060 --> 00:19:40,060
and then you have a dielectric coat,

323
00:19:40,060 --> 00:19:43,060
and then on the bottom you have a glass.

324
00:19:43,060 --> 00:19:46,060
So what's being shown here, again,

325
00:19:46,060 --> 00:19:49,060
is a complete structure that consists of the glass

326
00:19:49,060 --> 00:19:51,060
and then anti-reflective coating

327
00:19:51,060 --> 00:19:54,060
and finally the surrounding medium.

328
00:19:54,060 --> 00:19:56,060
So as the incident light is coming

329
00:19:56,060 --> 00:20:00,060
from the face-first medium, in this case, air,

330
00:20:00,060 --> 00:20:03,060
there's going to be a certain amount of reflection

331
00:20:03,060 --> 00:20:05,060
on the first frontal surface

332
00:20:05,060 --> 00:20:08,060
that's shown here as a reflected beam number one.

333
00:20:08,060 --> 00:20:13,060
And then most of the light is going to enter into the coating,

334
00:20:13,060 --> 00:20:16,060
and we can see that that transmitted light

335
00:20:16,060 --> 00:20:20,060
that entered the coating is going to be reflected again

336
00:20:20,060 --> 00:20:25,060
from the next surface between the coating and the glass.

337
00:20:25,060 --> 00:20:28,060
And so that reflection is going to come back

338
00:20:28,060 --> 00:20:33,060
and certain amount of it is going to pass back into the air.

339
00:20:33,060 --> 00:20:38,060
So we are going to end up with two reflected beams,

340
00:20:38,060 --> 00:20:42,060
the reflected beam one, or so-called primary reflected beam,

341
00:20:42,060 --> 00:20:44,060
and then reflected beam number two,

342
00:20:44,060 --> 00:20:46,060
or secondary reflected beam,

343
00:20:46,060 --> 00:20:49,060
that has been produced through the reflection

344
00:20:49,060 --> 00:20:52,060
between the optical coating and the glass.

345
00:20:52,060 --> 00:20:59,060
So these two reflected beams are going to interfere,

346
00:20:59,060 --> 00:21:03,060
and we're going to talk about that in lectures

347
00:21:03,060 --> 00:21:05,060
that follow this one.

348
00:21:05,060 --> 00:21:07,060
There's going to be certain, as I said, interference

349
00:21:07,060 --> 00:21:11,060
where these two guys will either be adding up

350
00:21:11,060 --> 00:21:14,060
or canceling out based on their phase,

351
00:21:14,060 --> 00:21:17,060
and this so-called phase is going to be controlled

352
00:21:17,060 --> 00:21:21,060
by the thickness of the optical coating.

353
00:21:21,060 --> 00:21:24,060
So the phase of the two reflected beams

354
00:21:24,060 --> 00:21:27,060
depends on their relative path lengths.

355
00:21:27,060 --> 00:21:30,060
We can see that the path length of the reflected beam one

356
00:21:30,060 --> 00:21:33,060
is significantly different than the path

357
00:21:33,060 --> 00:21:35,060
of the reflected beam number two,

358
00:21:35,060 --> 00:21:37,060
because reflected beam number two

359
00:21:37,060 --> 00:21:41,060
have this extra route to go through,

360
00:21:41,060 --> 00:21:44,060
which is equal to the approximately

361
00:21:44,060 --> 00:21:48,060
twice the length of the optical coating.

362
00:21:48,060 --> 00:21:50,060
So the path lengths are different,

363
00:21:50,060 --> 00:21:53,060
phases are different, and these phases are so adjusted

364
00:21:53,060 --> 00:21:57,060
for these two reflected beams to cancel out.

365
00:21:57,060 --> 00:22:01,060
In such a case, we have achieved

366
00:22:01,060 --> 00:22:04,060
a significant reduction of the reflection,

367
00:22:04,060 --> 00:22:06,060
or maybe to better use the term,

368
00:22:06,060 --> 00:22:08,060
instead of using the term reduction,

369
00:22:08,060 --> 00:22:13,060
better to use the term of cancellation of the reflections.

370
00:22:14,060 --> 00:22:19,060
So the reflection looks like it does not occur.

371
00:22:21,060 --> 00:22:24,060
So the effect can be seen on the picture

372
00:22:24,060 --> 00:22:25,060
on the right-hand side.

373
00:22:25,060 --> 00:22:29,060
We can see that the glasses

374
00:22:29,060 --> 00:22:32,060
on the picture on the left side

375
00:22:32,060 --> 00:22:34,060
don't have optical coating,

376
00:22:34,060 --> 00:22:36,060
so we see a significant reflection

377
00:22:36,060 --> 00:22:38,060
of the surface of the glass,

378
00:22:38,060 --> 00:22:41,060
as opposed to the optical glasses

379
00:22:41,060 --> 00:22:43,060
on the picture on the right-hand side,

380
00:22:43,060 --> 00:22:45,060
where there is no reflection,

381
00:22:45,060 --> 00:22:49,060
where the reflection has been significantly reduced

382
00:22:49,060 --> 00:22:54,060
to the process of entire reflection coating application.

383
00:22:54,060 --> 00:22:59,060
Obviously, the thickness of the optical coating

384
00:22:59,060 --> 00:23:01,060
is going to play an important role

385
00:23:01,060 --> 00:23:07,060
as far as how the reflection is going to be,

386
00:23:07,060 --> 00:23:09,060
what kind of interference is going to happen

387
00:23:09,060 --> 00:23:10,060
between the reflected waves,

388
00:23:10,060 --> 00:23:12,060
so there are certain formulas that can be used

389
00:23:12,060 --> 00:23:15,060
to calculate the thickness of the optical coating

390
00:23:15,060 --> 00:23:18,060
that's going to produce entire reflective properties.

391
00:23:18,060 --> 00:23:20,060
Specifically on this slide,

392
00:23:20,060 --> 00:23:25,060
you can see the whole concept being further expanded upon.

393
00:23:25,060 --> 00:23:27,060
So we are talking about, again,

394
00:23:27,060 --> 00:23:30,060
two reflected beams, beam number one

395
00:23:30,060 --> 00:23:32,060
and beam number two,

396
00:23:32,060 --> 00:23:38,060
that have a total of 180-degree phase difference

397
00:23:38,060 --> 00:23:42,060
that is going to enable so-called

398
00:23:42,060 --> 00:23:44,060
destructive interference or cancellation

399
00:23:44,060 --> 00:23:46,060
of these two reflected beams.

400
00:23:46,060 --> 00:23:48,060
There are certain formulas that can be used

401
00:23:48,060 --> 00:23:51,060
to calculate the thickness of this coating

402
00:23:51,060 --> 00:23:55,060
that is going to result in a 180-degree phase difference

403
00:23:55,060 --> 00:23:59,060
between the two phases of these two reflected beams,

404
00:23:59,060 --> 00:24:02,060
and if you do the proper math,

405
00:24:02,060 --> 00:24:03,060
you're coming up with a formula

406
00:24:03,060 --> 00:24:05,060
shown here on the right-hand side

407
00:24:05,060 --> 00:24:11,060
that can be used as a direction

408
00:24:11,060 --> 00:24:14,060
in terms of what kind of thickness

409
00:24:14,060 --> 00:24:20,060
would have to be selected for an optical coating

410
00:24:20,060 --> 00:24:23,060
to produce entire reflective properties

411
00:24:23,060 --> 00:24:28,060
at a specific single wavelength of light, lambda.

412
00:24:28,060 --> 00:24:30,060
Another popular type of coating

413
00:24:30,060 --> 00:24:33,060
is so-called high-reflection coating.

414
00:24:33,060 --> 00:24:37,060
So this type of coating has found many applications.

415
00:24:37,060 --> 00:24:40,060
This slide here shows a specific type of application,

416
00:24:40,060 --> 00:24:44,060
for example, in warmer regions of the country

417
00:24:44,060 --> 00:24:48,060
where you want to have good control of air conditioning.

418
00:24:48,060 --> 00:24:51,060
You don't want the solar energy

419
00:24:51,060 --> 00:25:00,060
to heat the interior spaces of apartments or houses.

420
00:25:00,060 --> 00:25:04,060
So you want to put a specific type of coating

421
00:25:04,060 --> 00:25:08,060
on the frontal surface of the windows

422
00:25:08,060 --> 00:25:11,060
that is going to enable a huge reflection

423
00:25:11,060 --> 00:25:15,060
of the light that is coming from the sun.

424
00:25:15,060 --> 00:25:19,060
So in such a case, you want to create

425
00:25:19,060 --> 00:25:22,060
the so-called high-reflection type of coating

426
00:25:22,060 --> 00:25:25,060
that is going to greatly increase the reflection.

427
00:25:25,060 --> 00:25:28,060
The way how this type of coating works

428
00:25:28,060 --> 00:25:32,060
is very similar to the previous anti-reflection coating.

429
00:25:32,060 --> 00:25:35,060
It's just that here the condition is different.

430
00:25:35,060 --> 00:25:38,060
So here we would want to establish

431
00:25:38,060 --> 00:25:41,060
or achieve the thickness of the coating

432
00:25:41,060 --> 00:25:45,060
that is going to enable the two reflective beams.

433
00:25:45,060 --> 00:25:47,060
If you remember our beam one and beam number two,

434
00:25:47,060 --> 00:25:50,060
you want these two beams this time

435
00:25:50,060 --> 00:25:52,060
to interfere constructively

436
00:25:52,060 --> 00:25:55,060
or you want them to add up.

437
00:25:55,060 --> 00:25:58,060
And here very often multiple layers

438
00:25:58,060 --> 00:26:00,060
of dielectric or metallic coatings

439
00:26:00,060 --> 00:26:03,060
are being placed on a glass or a suitable surface.

440
00:26:03,060 --> 00:26:06,060
You're going to see this type of application

441
00:26:06,060 --> 00:26:08,060
also very often in lasers

442
00:26:08,060 --> 00:26:11,060
because every laser has a cavity

443
00:26:11,060 --> 00:26:14,060
that ends with two mirrors on each side

444
00:26:14,060 --> 00:26:16,060
and those two mirrors have to have

445
00:26:16,060 --> 00:26:19,060
very, very good reflective properties.

446
00:26:19,060 --> 00:26:21,060
And the way how that's being achieved

447
00:26:21,060 --> 00:26:24,060
is by creating multiple layers of coating

448
00:26:24,060 --> 00:26:26,060
that is going to result

449
00:26:26,060 --> 00:26:31,060
in a very, very efficient type of reflection.

450
00:26:31,060 --> 00:26:33,060
So these coatings can be designed

451
00:26:33,060 --> 00:26:37,060
for very high reflectance or partial reflectance.

452
00:26:37,060 --> 00:26:41,060
It can be a function of wavelengths.

453
00:26:41,060 --> 00:26:44,060
And in the case of high reflection coatings,

454
00:26:44,060 --> 00:26:47,060
the thickness of the coating is, as I said,

455
00:26:47,060 --> 00:26:49,060
adjusted to give constructive interference

456
00:26:49,060 --> 00:26:52,060
between the reflected rays of light.

457
00:26:52,060 --> 00:26:53,060
High reflectivity can be achieved

458
00:26:53,060 --> 00:26:55,060
only in a narrow wavelength region

459
00:26:55,060 --> 00:26:58,060
and also depends on the angle of incidence.

460
00:26:58,060 --> 00:27:00,060
This is important, as I said, in the case of lasers

461
00:27:00,060 --> 00:27:03,060
because lasers are devices

462
00:27:03,060 --> 00:27:05,060
that are operating at a single wavelength.

463
00:27:05,060 --> 00:27:08,060
So using a specific type of coating

464
00:27:08,060 --> 00:27:11,060
is going to enable the reflection

465
00:27:11,060 --> 00:27:12,060
of that specific wavelength

466
00:27:12,060 --> 00:27:15,060
while other wavelengths are going to be passed

467
00:27:15,060 --> 00:27:16,060
through the mirrors

468
00:27:16,060 --> 00:27:18,060
as they are not playing an important role

469
00:27:18,060 --> 00:27:22,060
in the performance of a laser system.

470
00:27:22,060 --> 00:27:24,060
What you see on the right-hand side

471
00:27:24,060 --> 00:27:28,060
are properties of different types of materials

472
00:27:28,060 --> 00:27:30,060
based on the thickness of the layers

473
00:27:30,060 --> 00:27:31,060
that have been applied.

474
00:27:31,060 --> 00:27:33,060
And you can see here that

475
00:27:33,060 --> 00:27:36,060
that specific wavelength, lambda sub-zero,

476
00:27:36,060 --> 00:27:39,060
the reflectance is going to increase

477
00:27:39,060 --> 00:27:40,060
if you add more layers.

478
00:27:40,060 --> 00:27:43,060
So if you have applied just the two layers,

479
00:27:43,060 --> 00:27:46,060
the reflectance is around 99%

480
00:27:46,060 --> 00:27:47,060
and you can make it more efficient.

481
00:27:47,060 --> 00:27:48,060
You can make it more reflective

482
00:27:48,060 --> 00:27:50,060
by adding four or even five layers

483
00:27:50,060 --> 00:27:55,060
where the reflectance goes from 99%

484
00:27:55,060 --> 00:27:58,060
to almost 100%.

485
00:27:58,060 --> 00:28:02,060
Every photonic slab has plenty of mirrors.

486
00:28:02,060 --> 00:28:03,060
In the case of mirrors,

487
00:28:03,060 --> 00:28:05,060
we are using metallic coatings.

488
00:28:05,060 --> 00:28:09,060
So we are depositing certain coating

489
00:28:09,060 --> 00:28:12,060
that's made out of a metal.

490
00:28:12,060 --> 00:28:13,060
So in this case,

491
00:28:13,060 --> 00:28:15,060
we are talking about highly reflective,

492
00:28:15,060 --> 00:28:18,060
broadband, specular types of reflections,

493
00:28:18,060 --> 00:28:21,060
as opposed to the optical coatings

494
00:28:21,060 --> 00:28:25,060
made out of non-metallic materials

495
00:28:25,060 --> 00:28:28,060
where the reflection is focused

496
00:28:28,060 --> 00:28:30,060
on a specific single wavelength.

497
00:28:30,060 --> 00:28:32,060
Here, we can produce the reflections

498
00:28:32,060 --> 00:28:36,060
at a large range of different wavelengths.

499
00:28:36,060 --> 00:28:38,060
And you can see here different types of metals

500
00:28:38,060 --> 00:28:41,060
that are being used to produce metallic coatings,

501
00:28:41,060 --> 00:28:43,060
aluminum, gold.

502
00:28:43,060 --> 00:28:45,060
And it's very important to mention

503
00:28:45,060 --> 00:28:47,060
that these metals are sensitive to corrosion

504
00:28:47,060 --> 00:28:49,060
and therefore must be protected

505
00:28:49,060 --> 00:28:52,060
by an additional protective dielectric coating.

506
00:28:52,060 --> 00:28:55,060
Those nuances are addressed in some way

507
00:28:55,060 --> 00:28:58,060
during the production of the mirrors

508
00:28:58,060 --> 00:29:01,060
at the facilities that are making mirrors.

509
00:29:01,060 --> 00:29:03,060
There's a few things that you would want

510
00:29:03,060 --> 00:29:06,060
to pay close attention to.

511
00:29:06,060 --> 00:29:08,060
You can see that, for example, aluminum

512
00:29:08,060 --> 00:29:11,060
has relatively good properties

513
00:29:11,060 --> 00:29:14,060
in terms of reflectance over the entire

514
00:29:14,060 --> 00:29:17,060
ultraviolet and visible

515
00:29:17,060 --> 00:29:22,060
and even infrared range that is about 90%.

516
00:29:22,060 --> 00:29:24,060
As opposed to, for example, gold,

517
00:29:24,060 --> 00:29:26,060
where you can see that the reflectance of gold

518
00:29:26,060 --> 00:29:29,060
is good in infrared region about 700 nanometers,

519
00:29:29,060 --> 00:29:32,060
while as you are moving into the visible region,

520
00:29:32,060 --> 00:29:35,060
the reflectance of gold is kind of dropping down

521
00:29:35,060 --> 00:29:40,060
to almost 40% at a color of violet

522
00:29:40,060 --> 00:29:43,060
of 400 nanometers.

523
00:29:43,060 --> 00:29:46,060
Let's slightly change the subject here

524
00:29:46,060 --> 00:29:48,060
after we talked about coatings.

525
00:29:48,060 --> 00:29:50,060
We also want to introduce optical filters.

526
00:29:50,060 --> 00:29:53,060
Optical filters are also components

527
00:29:53,060 --> 00:29:58,060
that are very frequently used in photonics labs.

528
00:29:58,060 --> 00:30:01,060
You may come across many different types of filters

529
00:30:01,060 --> 00:30:05,060
and here the classification is being presented.

530
00:30:05,060 --> 00:30:07,060
So you may come across so-called

531
00:30:07,060 --> 00:30:10,060
broadband filters, narrowband filters,

532
00:30:10,060 --> 00:30:13,060
ND or neutral density filters,

533
00:30:13,060 --> 00:30:14,060
stepped filters, radiometric,

534
00:30:14,060 --> 00:30:17,060
photometric filters, polarizing filters,

535
00:30:17,060 --> 00:30:21,060
and also certain special application filters.

536
00:30:21,060 --> 00:30:23,060
In the following slides,

537
00:30:23,060 --> 00:30:28,060
we're going to talk about some of the most popular

538
00:30:28,060 --> 00:30:32,060
filters from this list.

539
00:30:32,060 --> 00:30:35,060
The first type of filter that we're going to talk about

540
00:30:35,060 --> 00:30:36,060
are so-called broadband filters.

541
00:30:36,060 --> 00:30:39,060
Here we are talking about the filters that can transmit

542
00:30:39,060 --> 00:30:41,060
a certain band of wavelengths of light

543
00:30:41,060 --> 00:30:45,060
while blocking all other wavelengths outside of this band.

544
00:30:45,060 --> 00:30:49,060
A typical performance curve of this type of broadband filter

545
00:30:49,060 --> 00:30:51,060
is shown here on this slide.

546
00:30:51,060 --> 00:30:55,060
You can see a bell-shaped type of a curve.

547
00:30:55,060 --> 00:30:58,060
What this bell-shaped or Gaussian type of a curl

548
00:30:58,060 --> 00:31:01,060
represents is basically what kind of wavelengths

549
00:31:01,060 --> 00:31:05,060
are being passed to this type of a broadband filter.

550
00:31:05,060 --> 00:31:07,060
So at the peak of the bell-shaped curve

551
00:31:07,060 --> 00:31:09,060
is your central wavelength.

552
00:31:09,060 --> 00:31:11,060
That's where you have the most of transmittance.

553
00:31:11,060 --> 00:31:14,060
So if you select that wavelength,

554
00:31:14,060 --> 00:31:16,060
most of the light at that wavelength

555
00:31:16,060 --> 00:31:18,060
is going to be transmitted to this type of filter.

556
00:31:18,060 --> 00:31:21,060
As you're moving away from the central wavelength,

557
00:31:21,060 --> 00:31:24,060
you still have a certain range of wavelengths

558
00:31:24,060 --> 00:31:26,060
around that central wavelength

559
00:31:26,060 --> 00:31:29,060
where the transmittance is relatively high.

560
00:31:29,060 --> 00:31:31,060
The way how you are establishing

561
00:31:31,060 --> 00:31:34,060
so-called bandwidth of a broadband filter

562
00:31:34,060 --> 00:31:37,060
is you're basically looking at the entire range

563
00:31:37,060 --> 00:31:40,060
of the wavelengths where the transmittance

564
00:31:40,060 --> 00:31:43,060
of a specific wavelength is about 50%

565
00:31:43,060 --> 00:31:45,060
of the transmittance of the peak.

566
00:31:45,060 --> 00:31:49,060
So, for example, if the peak has transmittance

567
00:31:49,060 --> 00:31:52,060
of, let's say, 94%,

568
00:31:52,060 --> 00:31:55,060
you're going to be looking at 50% of 94,

569
00:31:55,060 --> 00:31:58,060
which in our case is going to be 47.

570
00:31:58,060 --> 00:32:01,060
So at 47, you're going to look at the two wavelengths

571
00:32:01,060 --> 00:32:07,060
where the transmittance is going to be dropped down to 47,

572
00:32:07,060 --> 00:32:10,060
and you're going to establish the lower

573
00:32:10,060 --> 00:32:13,060
and then higher end of your passband,

574
00:32:13,060 --> 00:32:15,060
and that's going to define the bandwidth

575
00:32:15,060 --> 00:32:18,060
of the broadband filter.

576
00:32:18,060 --> 00:32:19,060
Another type of filter to mention

577
00:32:19,060 --> 00:32:21,060
are so-called cutoff filters.

578
00:32:21,060 --> 00:32:23,060
So these filters would allow radiation

579
00:32:23,060 --> 00:32:26,060
up to a specific wavelength to pass

580
00:32:26,060 --> 00:32:30,060
while cutting all other remaining wavelengths.

581
00:32:30,060 --> 00:32:34,060
So for this specific type of curve that we're seeing here,

582
00:32:34,060 --> 00:32:37,060
you can see that transmittance is very high,

583
00:32:37,060 --> 00:32:40,060
is about 90% for all the wavelengths

584
00:32:40,060 --> 00:32:43,060
above about 650 nanometers.

585
00:32:43,060 --> 00:32:45,060
So this kind of filter is going to be passing

586
00:32:45,060 --> 00:32:48,060
almost the entire infrared range

587
00:32:48,060 --> 00:32:50,060
while cutting all the wavelengths

588
00:32:50,060 --> 00:32:53,060
below 650 nanometers into the visible range

589
00:32:53,060 --> 00:32:56,060
and further into the ultraviolet range.

590
00:32:56,060 --> 00:32:57,060
We're going to find many applications

591
00:32:57,060 --> 00:32:59,060
of these types of filters.

592
00:32:59,060 --> 00:33:04,060
For example, if you're dealing with a specific type of a laser,

593
00:33:04,060 --> 00:33:08,060
you want to address the safety concerns.

594
00:33:08,060 --> 00:33:11,060
That laser is going to have a certain harmful radiation

595
00:33:11,060 --> 00:33:14,060
at certain wavelengths,

596
00:33:14,060 --> 00:33:17,060
so you would have to use a specific type of a filter

597
00:33:17,060 --> 00:33:19,060
to cut off those wavelengths

598
00:33:19,060 --> 00:33:23,060
so that you don't harm the eye

599
00:33:23,060 --> 00:33:24,060
of the photonic technician

600
00:33:24,060 --> 00:33:26,060
that is working with that type of a laser.

601
00:33:26,060 --> 00:33:30,060
So specifically here, frequency double YAG laser

602
00:33:30,060 --> 00:33:34,060
is mentioned that has output of 532 nanometers

603
00:33:34,060 --> 00:33:38,060
and then also harmful infrared radiation

604
00:33:38,060 --> 00:33:41,060
at 10, 60 nanometers.

605
00:33:41,060 --> 00:33:46,060
So very often we would want to cut some of these wavelengths

606
00:33:46,060 --> 00:33:52,060
in order to block any kind of potential damage

607
00:33:52,060 --> 00:33:56,060
to either optical system

608
00:33:56,060 --> 00:34:02,060
or to reduce the risk of any kind of hazard

609
00:34:02,060 --> 00:34:06,060
to the people who are working in photonics lab.

610
00:34:06,060 --> 00:34:08,060
Neutral density filters,

611
00:34:08,060 --> 00:34:11,060
filters are the filters that are not wavelength sensitive.

612
00:34:11,060 --> 00:34:13,060
They're intensity sensitive.

613
00:34:13,060 --> 00:34:15,060
So we can see on the curves on the right-hand side

614
00:34:15,060 --> 00:34:18,060
that the performance of these filters

615
00:34:18,060 --> 00:34:22,060
is relatively flat over the entire range of wavelengths.

616
00:34:22,060 --> 00:34:27,060
And these filters are also going to be very, very, very frequently used

617
00:34:27,060 --> 00:34:28,060
in an optical lab.

618
00:34:28,060 --> 00:34:31,060
So for example, you may have a certain equipment,

619
00:34:31,060 --> 00:34:33,060
certain measurement equipment

620
00:34:33,060 --> 00:34:36,060
that is sensitive to the irradiance

621
00:34:36,060 --> 00:34:42,060
or the power of the light that is entering their detectors.

622
00:34:42,060 --> 00:34:45,060
So you would want to put this type of filter

623
00:34:45,060 --> 00:34:47,060
to reduce the intensity of light

624
00:34:47,060 --> 00:34:51,060
so that it becomes non-harmful to the detectors

625
00:34:51,060 --> 00:34:55,060
that are being used in these measuring devices.

626
00:34:55,060 --> 00:34:59,060
You can see that according to the label

627
00:34:59,060 --> 00:35:03,060
you may have ND30, ND20, ND10 or ND5

628
00:35:03,060 --> 00:35:05,060
and many others.

629
00:35:05,060 --> 00:35:07,060
And each of these different labels

630
00:35:07,060 --> 00:35:14,060
would correspond to a specific amount of transmittance

631
00:35:14,060 --> 00:35:18,060
through that type of neutral density filter.

632
00:35:18,060 --> 00:35:23,060
The type of the filter depends also on something called optical density

633
00:35:23,060 --> 00:35:26,060
or so-called number OD.

634
00:35:26,060 --> 00:35:30,060
And we can see that the OD as shown on a plot

635
00:35:30,060 --> 00:35:31,060
on the right-hand side

636
00:35:31,060 --> 00:35:34,060
for different types of neutral density filter

637
00:35:34,060 --> 00:35:38,060
can be translated into a specific amount of transmittance

638
00:35:38,060 --> 00:35:43,060
using the formula shown in the equation on the left-hand side.

639
00:35:43,060 --> 00:35:51,060
So for example, if you are considering the neutral density filter ND20

640
00:35:51,060 --> 00:35:57,060
you can see that ND20 would have an optical density of about 2.

641
00:35:57,060 --> 00:36:00,060
So ND20 has an optical density of about 2.

642
00:36:00,060 --> 00:36:04,060
So if you take the OD equal to 2

643
00:36:04,060 --> 00:36:08,060
and you substitute in the formula on the left-hand side

644
00:36:08,060 --> 00:36:11,060
you're going to have 10 to the negative 2

645
00:36:11,060 --> 00:36:14,060
which is equal to 0.01 or 1%.

646
00:36:14,060 --> 00:36:17,060
In other words, that type of a filter is going to be passing

647
00:36:17,060 --> 00:36:20,060
only 1% of the incident light.

648
00:36:20,060 --> 00:36:26,060
So the ND20 filter is going to be passing only 1% of the incident light

649
00:36:26,060 --> 00:36:31,060
while blocking the rest of 99% of that light.

650
00:36:31,060 --> 00:36:35,060
So that's how you can translate a specific label

651
00:36:35,060 --> 00:36:39,060
or type of neutral density filter based on the curve

652
00:36:39,060 --> 00:36:41,060
that you see on the right-hand side

653
00:36:41,060 --> 00:36:44,060
into a specific amount of transmittance.

654
00:36:44,060 --> 00:36:47,060
Let's mention a few other types of filters.

655
00:36:47,060 --> 00:36:50,060
Narrow band filter is a type of filter

656
00:36:50,060 --> 00:36:53,060
that's going to pass the light only at a specific wavelength

657
00:36:53,060 --> 00:36:57,060
while the rest of the spectrum is going to get reflected.

658
00:36:57,060 --> 00:37:00,060
So it's kind of like very similar to a broadband filter

659
00:37:00,060 --> 00:37:03,060
is just that in this case the whole bandwidth

660
00:37:03,060 --> 00:37:07,060
is very, very, very reduced to a minimum

661
00:37:07,060 --> 00:37:09,060
almost like a single wavelength is being passed

662
00:37:09,060 --> 00:37:10,060
through this type of a filter.

663
00:37:10,060 --> 00:37:12,060
Radiometric filters are the filters

664
00:37:12,060 --> 00:37:15,060
that are going to transmit light equally at all wavelengths.

665
00:37:15,060 --> 00:37:18,060
They have found different applications

666
00:37:18,060 --> 00:37:20,060
such as in the case of light detectors

667
00:37:20,060 --> 00:37:23,060
that often do not have a flat response

668
00:37:23,060 --> 00:37:24,060
to all the wavelengths of light.

669
00:37:24,060 --> 00:37:26,060
And these filters are going to be used

670
00:37:26,060 --> 00:37:29,060
to establish that kind of balance

671
00:37:29,060 --> 00:37:32,060
so that the light detector reacts

672
00:37:32,060 --> 00:37:37,060
to the entire range of wavelengths of interest in the same way.

673
00:37:37,060 --> 00:37:39,060
Photometric filters are the filters

674
00:37:39,060 --> 00:37:43,060
that are transmitting maximum light at 550 nanometers

675
00:37:43,060 --> 00:37:46,060
and then falls deeply on each side.

676
00:37:46,060 --> 00:37:49,060
And these type of photometric filters

677
00:37:49,060 --> 00:37:52,060
are basically a replica of the performance of a human eye.

678
00:37:52,060 --> 00:37:55,060
So you can see the two curves on the left-hand side

679
00:37:55,060 --> 00:38:00,060
one that represents the filtering properties of a human eye

680
00:38:00,060 --> 00:38:04,060
another one that represents the filtering properties

681
00:38:04,060 --> 00:38:06,060
of a photocell with a photometric filter.

682
00:38:06,060 --> 00:38:11,060
So there's certain applications where you want to replicate

683
00:38:11,060 --> 00:38:14,060
the filtering properties of the human eye

684
00:38:14,060 --> 00:38:16,060
and that's where the photometric filters

685
00:38:16,060 --> 00:38:18,060
are going to find their use.

686
00:38:18,060 --> 00:38:22,060
And finally, at the end, since we are talking about the filters

687
00:38:22,060 --> 00:38:26,060
we cannot avoid the subject of a protective eyewear.

688
00:38:26,060 --> 00:38:29,060
This is an important subject that is going to be addressed

689
00:38:29,060 --> 00:38:33,060
in a separate lecture at the end of this course.

690
00:38:33,060 --> 00:38:37,060
We are going to soon talk about the human eye

691
00:38:37,060 --> 00:38:41,060
and its sensitivity to the light

692
00:38:41,060 --> 00:38:45,060
especially to a stronger power of the light beams.

693
00:38:45,060 --> 00:38:49,060
So before we talk about that we're going to just

694
00:38:49,060 --> 00:38:51,060
introduce the concept of the laser safety

695
00:38:51,060 --> 00:38:54,060
and the safety in the photonics lab.

696
00:38:54,060 --> 00:38:57,060
We are going to see that certain lasers

697
00:38:57,060 --> 00:39:00,060
with the powers above certain thresholds

698
00:39:00,060 --> 00:39:02,060
and the certain durations of exposure

699
00:39:02,060 --> 00:39:05,060
will be having a hazardous effect

700
00:39:05,060 --> 00:39:08,060
harmful effect to the human eye.

701
00:39:08,060 --> 00:39:13,060
So as a photonics technician you would need to know

702
00:39:13,060 --> 00:39:16,060
how to properly select a protective safety eyewear

703
00:39:16,060 --> 00:39:18,060
in the photonics lab.

704
00:39:18,060 --> 00:39:21,060
So again, laser safety is a very important factor

705
00:39:21,060 --> 00:39:24,060
in the laser laboratory and this protective eyewear

706
00:39:24,060 --> 00:39:28,060
is nothing else than a specific type of filter

707
00:39:28,060 --> 00:39:31,060
that is blocking light at specific wavelengths

708
00:39:31,060 --> 00:39:34,060
while passing the light at other wavelengths.

709
00:39:34,060 --> 00:39:37,060
So if you're looking at a human body

710
00:39:37,060 --> 00:39:40,060
with various organs it turns out that the eye

711
00:39:40,060 --> 00:39:43,060
is the most vulnerable to the laser radiation

712
00:39:43,060 --> 00:39:46,060
because if the reflected laser beam

713
00:39:46,060 --> 00:39:51,060
or incident laser beam is focused on a retina of the eye

714
00:39:51,060 --> 00:39:54,060
it can result in a significant damage

715
00:39:54,060 --> 00:39:57,060
that may be severe and irreparable.

716
00:39:57,060 --> 00:40:00,060
And in order for that to avoid

717
00:40:00,060 --> 00:40:03,060
you have to use specific type of a protective eyewear,

718
00:40:03,060 --> 00:40:06,060
specific type of safety goggles

719
00:40:06,060 --> 00:40:09,060
that would have specific types of properties

720
00:40:09,060 --> 00:40:12,060
and specific type of filtering characteristics.

721
00:40:12,060 --> 00:40:16,060
So you can see here one type of laser protective eyewear

722
00:40:16,060 --> 00:40:19,060
and then also a curve that presents the transmittance

723
00:40:19,060 --> 00:40:22,060
through this type of laser safety eyewear.

724
00:40:22,060 --> 00:40:27,060
You can see here that this type of laser safety eyewear

725
00:40:27,060 --> 00:40:30,060
is going to have good blocking characteristics

726
00:40:30,060 --> 00:40:34,060
at the wavelengths of 430 and below.

727
00:40:34,060 --> 00:40:37,060
So this type of laser safety eyewear

728
00:40:37,060 --> 00:40:41,060
is going to be used for that range, for the ultraviolet range

729
00:40:41,060 --> 00:40:44,060
while at the same time is passing the visible range.

730
00:40:44,060 --> 00:40:46,060
So if you are dealing with, for example,

731
00:40:46,060 --> 00:40:49,060
the ultraviolet laser, UV laser

732
00:40:49,060 --> 00:40:53,060
you want to protect your eyes from that UV radiation

733
00:40:53,060 --> 00:40:55,060
that's coming off of the UV laser

734
00:40:55,060 --> 00:40:58,060
and this type of laser safety eyewear

735
00:40:58,060 --> 00:41:00,060
with this type of filtering curve

736
00:41:00,060 --> 00:41:04,060
may be a good fit for that type of purpose

737
00:41:04,060 --> 00:41:06,060
or for that type of application.

738
00:41:06,060 --> 00:41:10,060
To conclude the lecture number five of our video series,

739
00:41:10,060 --> 00:41:15,060
in this lecture we started considering certain practical aspects

740
00:41:15,060 --> 00:41:21,060
of photonics lab after we introduced light

741
00:41:21,060 --> 00:41:24,060
and important quantities that describes the light

742
00:41:24,060 --> 00:41:26,060
in our first three lectures

743
00:41:26,060 --> 00:41:31,060
and after we laid out the foundation of photonics

744
00:41:31,060 --> 00:41:34,060
and after we talked about the terminology of light,

745
00:41:34,060 --> 00:41:38,060
the nature of light, its interaction with the media.

746
00:41:38,060 --> 00:41:43,060
In this lecture we are slowly moving into the photonics lab

747
00:41:43,060 --> 00:41:48,060
and we are trying to see how all those basic foundations

748
00:41:48,060 --> 00:41:51,060
of how the light interacts with the media

749
00:41:51,060 --> 00:41:55,060
can be translated into its use in the photonics lab.

750
00:41:55,060 --> 00:41:58,060
So we started talking about optical materials,

751
00:41:58,060 --> 00:42:02,060
optical components, coatings, filters.

752
00:42:03,060 --> 00:42:06,060
So these types of components

753
00:42:06,060 --> 00:42:09,060
are going to be frequently used in the photonics lab.

754
00:42:09,060 --> 00:42:11,060
So you're going to be frequently using them

755
00:42:11,060 --> 00:42:12,060
as a photonics technician.

756
00:42:12,060 --> 00:42:15,060
So it's important to understand the background

757
00:42:15,060 --> 00:42:18,060
behind their operation.

758
00:42:18,060 --> 00:42:19,060
Next time in the lecture six,

759
00:42:19,060 --> 00:42:21,060
we are going to be talking about optical amounts

760
00:42:21,060 --> 00:42:26,060
which is also going to be an important subject

761
00:42:26,060 --> 00:42:30,060
that you should be aware as a photonics technician.

762
00:42:30,060 --> 00:42:33,060
So here I'm going to conclude lecture number five.

763
00:42:33,060 --> 00:42:36,060
I wish you the pleasant rest of the day

